answer:
The EU AI Act lists specific serious criminal offences that can justify using real-time remote biometric identification to find or identify suspects [Art. 5(1)(h)], [Annex II]. 

The offences on this list are [Annex II]:
* Terrorism, trafficking in human beings, sexual exploitation of children and child pornography, kidnapping, illegal restraint, hostage-taking, and rape [Annex II];
* Murder, grievous bodily injury, and crimes within the jurisdiction of the International Criminal Court [Annex II];
* Illicit trafficking in narcotic drugs, psychotropic substances, weapons, munitions, explosives, human organs or tissue, and nuclear or radioactive materials [Annex II];
* Environmental crime, organised or armed robbery, sabotage, unlawful seizure of aircraft or ships, and participation in a criminal organisation involved in any of these crimes [Annex II].

To permit this biometric use, the offence must also be punishable in the relevant Member State by a prison sentence or detention order of at least four years [Art. 5(1)(h)], [Recital 33].

The AI Act establishes shared rules across the European Union to regulate artificial intelligence, ban harmful practices, and support innovation [Art. 1(2)]. 

The Act specifically sets out:
* Standard rules for selling, putting into service, and using AI systems—machine-based systems that can act with autonomy to generate outputs like predictions, content, or decisions [Art. 1(2)(a)], [Art. 3(1)];
* Bans on specific AI practices and distinct requirements for high-risk AI systems, including duties for the people or organizations that operate them [Art. 1(2)(b)], [Art. 1(2)(c)];
* Transparency requirements for certain AI systems and shared rules for selling general-purpose AI models [Art. 1(2)(d)], [Art. 1(2)(e)];
* Rules covering governance, monitoring, and enforcement—including creating a European Artificial Intelligence Board—as well as measures to foster innovation with a focus on small and medium-sized businesses and start-ups [Art. 1(2)(f)], [Art. 1(2)(g)], [Art. 65(1)].
